  "prediction": "**Rescue Mission: Swedish Professor Helps Save Student's Family from ISIS**\n\nIn a remarkable story of courage and determination, Swedish professor Charlotta Turner helped rescue her graduate student Firas Jumaah's family from the clutches of ISIS in Iraq in 2014. Jumaah, a member of the Yazidi minority, had been working on his PhD at Lund University in southern Sweden when he received a distressing text message from his wife, informing him that ISIS fighters had taken over their village and were killing men and enslaving women.\n\nJumaah immediately returned to Iraq to be with his family, but soon found himself in a desperate situation. With ISIS advancing on their location, Jumaah sent a text message to his supervisor, Professor Turner, asking her to remove him from the doctoral program if he wasn't back in Sweden within a week. However, Turner was not willing to leave her student to die without trying to do something.\n\nWhen Turner called Jumaah, she was shocked to hear the desperation in his voice. She quickly realized that his family was facing a life-and-death situation and decided to take action. Turner contacted the university's security chief, Per Gustafson, who arranged for a security company to rescue Jumaah and his family.\n\nOver a few days of intense activity, Gustafson hired a security company that arranged a rescue operation. Two Landcruisers carrying four heavily-armed mercenaries roared into the area where Jumaah was hiding, and sped him away to Erbil Airport together with his wife and two small children. Jumaah was later reunited with his family in Sweden, where he completed his PhD and now works for a pharmaceuticals company in Malmö.\n\nThe rescue operation was a unique event, with no other university involved in a similar mission. Gustafson described it as \"a unique event\" and Turner said that she was \"obvious\" that she should help and bring Jumaah's family home. The family has almost finished paying the university back for the rescue operation, and Jumaah has expressed his gratitude to Turner, saying that he felt \"privileged\" and \"VIP\" during the rescue, but also like a \"coward\" for leaving his mother and sisters behind.\n\nThis remarkable story highlights the bravery and determination of Professor Turner and the university's security team, who risked their lives to save Jumaah's family from the clutches of ISIS. It also serves as a testament to the importance of human connection and the power of one person to make a difference",
